INT. OPERA HOUSE - FLY GALLERY - NIGHT

A narrow catwalk above the stage. Ropes and sandbags, counterweights, a chandelier cable thick as an arm. Below, a performance of "La Traviata" swells through the dark.

SABINE ORTEGA, 40s, in black, crouches at a winch with a glass cutter. CALLUM REID, 30s, hangs from a harness, a duffel at his hip. WANJIRU KARIUKI, 20s, the new hire, grips the rail, eyes huge.

SABINE
Quiet. Quiet.

CALLUM
I am quiet.

WANJIRU
(whispering)
Is it supposed to be this loud down there?

SABINE
That's the opera.

WANJIRU
It's very opera.

SABINE
Cut the cable on my signal.

She raises one gloved hand. Below, the soprano reaches a trembling high note. The audience gasps.

SABINE (CONT'D)
Now.

CALLUM
Wait, wait, wait.

WANJIRU
Wait?

CALLUM
SABINE
(speaking at once)
There's a guard.
(speaking at once)
There's no guard.

They freeze. Far below, a flashlight beam slides across the stage and up through the rigging. It stops on the chandelier. It stops on them.

She slides the cutter into her belt. He shifts his weight in the harness, the whole rig swaying. She looks at Callum. She looks at Wanjiru. Then she looks at the cable.

SABINE (CONT'D)
(a breath)
Cut it anyway.

WANJIRU
But--

SABINE
Do it.

Wanjiru closes her eyes, takes the cutter, and severs the cable. The chandelier drops. The audience screams.

Everything goes dark.
